    "q-185004": {
      "dom_id": "step-q185004",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20),$ then $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$ for any $a,b\\in Z.$ \n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be a pair of integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-2}}(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nSince this statement is false, its negation is true.\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be a pair of integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
