    "q-221741": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221741",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a\\in Z,$ if $9∣a$ and $15∣a,$ then $135∣a.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find  {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ {{field-7}}.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n9{{field-8}}45\\,\\text{and}\\,15{{field-9}}45\\,\\text{and}\\,135{{field-10}}45.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ {{field-11}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "worked_solution": "By denoting $P(a),$ $Q(a),$ and $R(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,9∣a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,15∣a$\n\n- $R(a):\\,135∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)⇒R.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)⇒R) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬((P∧Q)⇒R) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬(P∧Q)∨R) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)∧¬R \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧Q∧¬R \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(9∣a)\\,∧\\,(15∣a)\\,∧\\,(135∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n9∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,135∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=45$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=45.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n9∣45\\,\\text{and}\\,15∣45\\,\\text{and}\\,135∤45.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=45$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.\n\nPROOF\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find  a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that\n\n$$\n9∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,135∤a.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ $45$.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n9∣45\\,\\text{and}\\,15∣45\\,\\text{and}\\,135∤45.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ $45$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
