    "q-221748": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221748",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ then $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThen, $6a=$ {{field-8}} and $6b=$ {{field-9}}. \n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}{{field-10}}{{60}_{⏟}}_{6b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{10}_{⏟}}_{2a}{{field-11}}{{20}_{⏟}}_{2b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-12}} and $b=$ {{field-13}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "worked_solution": "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15))\\,∧\\,(2a≢2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\,\\text{and}\\,2a≢2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(5,10)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=5,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nThen, $6a=30$ and $6b=60.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}≡{{60}_{⏟}}_{6b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{10}_{⏟}}_{2a}≢{{20}_{⏟}}_{2b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=5$ and $b=10$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.\n\nPROOF\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n6a≡6b(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\,\\text{and}\\,2a≢2b(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=5,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nThen, $6a=$ $30$ and $6b=$ $60$. \n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}≡{{60}_{⏟}}_{6b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{10}_{⏟}}_{2a}≢{{20}_{⏟}}_{2b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ $5$ and $b=$ $10$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
